# INTENTIONAL DIVERGENCE (a compile error, so there is no runtime output to
# oracle). `f(a=1, a=2)` used to be accepted, collect its keywords into a dict,
# and silently keep the last — an argument the caller wrote, dropped without a
# word. It is refused at compile time now, where the other argument-rule
# refusals live. CPython also refuses it, at parse time, with a different
# message.
def connect(host, timeout=30):
    return host

print(connect("h", timeout=1, timeout=2))
